        "thinking": "OK now I have a clear picture. Let me plan a comprehensive implementation:\n\n**Key challenges:**\n1. Route factories return Express Routers, not `RouteDefinition[]`\n2. Some route files use `router.param()` for ID resolution (agents, issues, projects)\n3. Some handlers need Express-specific features (multer, SSE, scoped API)\n\n**The approach I'll take:**\n\nRather than trying to extract handlers from Express Router internals, I'll take a **hybrid approach**:\n\n1. **Keep the Node/Express path as-is** - nothing changes there\n\n\n2. **For the CF Worker, implement a separate routing layer** that directly imports and calls the underlying service functions using Hono routing, resolving auth natively\n\nThis means some route duplication in the CF Worker, but the handlers themselves stay transport-agnostic. Instead of re-implementing routes, I could create a dual-mount pattern where each route file exports both an Express router and a Hono router, keeping the actual business logic DRY while adapting the transport layer for each platform.\n\nRather than refactoring all 25 route files at once, I'll take a phased approach: first extract routes from the Express router using a proxy-based utility for the CF Worker, then gradually migrate to the dual-mount pattern. This means modifying the Express adapter to tag handlers, creating utilities to extract and remount routes on Hono, and implementing auth middleware for the CF Worker.\n\nNow I'll start implementing the core pieces: updating the Express adapter to support route extraction, building the Hono router utility, adding the auth resolution middleware, and fixing the type definitions.\n\nNext I need to implement the remaining handlers for cron jobs and R2 uploads, then wire everything together in the main worker API file. Let me first check how the main server mounts its routes so I can replicate that structure for the CF Worker.",
